{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that 'no claim about the future is made' contradicts the problem's explicit premise that scientists 'prefer' the theory 'when choosing which theory to act on.' Acting on a theory for high-stakes prediction inherently entails an expectation of future reliability; describing this merely as a 'report of test history' fails to address the pragmatic induction smuggled into the decision to act, thereby failing to answer whether the preference is defensible without induction.","premise":"The problem states scientists prefer the better-corroborated theory specifically when 'choosing which theory to act on,' implying a forward-looking expectation of success.","premises_essential":["bc95105f2a24bb5abab5900751b599e767ab05a7fe9b041378f128c0b1739cfc"],"successor_question":"If the preference implies no future guarantees, on what rational basis does Popper advise scientists to act on the corroborated theory rather than a falsified one for future applications?","target":"bc95105f2a24bb5abab5900751b599e767ab05a7fe9b041378f128c0b1739cfc"}]}